IMPLEMENTED COMPACT-CATALOG RULES
=================================
Indoor nonhousing use area <= two tower floor equivalents (12,000 sq ft).
This is an aggregate per-use bound; repeats cannot bypass it.
Housing minimum is five whole apartments. Current housing defaults are five.
Ground-floor uses and outdoor uses have separate scenario area caps.
Hospital, school, full supermarket and outdoor sports are excluded from active choices.
Training services are narrowed; facility counts remain whole.
Tower area is capped at 30 stories (180,000 sq ft), independently of bonuses.
The separate podium remains capped at five stories. Net-to-gross conversion remains unset.
Historical checklist below: implemented rules above supersede older defaults.

MISSING CONSTRAINTS AND OPTIMIZATION DECISIONS
============================================

Scope: development optimization using the active 33-use dummy catalog.
All decisions below remain unspecified. No numeric limits are assumed here.
Existing example program sizes and financial rates are inputs, not parcel limits.

1. Parcel definitions and baseline buildable capacity
---------------------------------------------------
- Define the parcels available for development and each parcel's land area.
- Specify base FAR and the land-area denominator used to calculate FAR.
- Specify any existing floor area that consumes available capacity.
- Identify which parcels permit towers and which are plinth-only.
- Specify an absolute physical/entitlement limit on total floor area, even when
  amenities earn FAR bonuses.

2. Dedicated open-space capacity
-------------------------------
- Specify the open-space allotment available on each parcel or across the site.
- Define explicitly which uses consume that allotment; the active outdoor uses
  are park, playground, and outdoor_sports.
- Determine whether allotments can be shared or transferred between parcels.
- Enforce outdoor area <= available dedicated open-space area.
- Open-space amenities must not consume indoor buildable floor capacity.
- Do not apply the withdrawn four-floor open-space bonus proposal.

3. Construction and development budget
--------------------------------------
- Specify a maximum budget, if spending is to be constrained.
- Decide whether the budget covers construction alone or total development.
- Count hard and soft construction costs, including public amenities and outdoor
  site improvements, for the selected program and its plinth/tower placement.
- If land belongs in the budget, count it once per acquired parcel rather than
  once per amenity. Specify whether existing land is already owned/sunk cost.
- Identify who pays public/community construction and maintenance costs.
- Specify whether annual maintenance has a separate funding/budget limit.
- Financing, parking, and other project costs require explicit scope decisions
  if they are to be included; the dummy catalog does not currently price them.

4. Decision variables, quantities, and divisibility
-------------------------------------------------
- Decide whether choices are integer dwelling units, commercial bays, whole
  facilities, continuous square footage, or a combination by program_unit.
- Define how quantities convert into allocated area using default_unit_sqft.
- Decide which facilities can be resized and which have fixed footprints.
- Decide whether a use can appear multiple times within or across parcels.
- Specify any maximum counts of facilities, units, or bays.
- Replace example square_footage with actual selected area in all calculations.

5. Selected-program minimums and maximums
----------------------------------------
- Enforce the existing minimum_program_sqft and maximum_program_sqft when a
  use is selected: zero if absent, otherwise within its permitted size range.
- Decide whether these bounds apply per facility, per parcel, or to the total
  development. Their current documented meaning is total use within a development.
- Check consistency between integer quantities and area bounds.
- These are physical/program constraints only. There is no subsidy eligibility
  cutoff; bonuses remain proportional to size.

6. Building form, placement, and usable capacity
----------------------------------------------
- Specify plinth capacity, tower capacity, and available ground-floor area.
- Specify building footprints, floor counts/heights, and any coverage limits
  needed to derive those capacities.
- Define whether ground_level means mandatory ground-floor placement.
- Enforce tower_allowed restrictions and any additional use/placement rules.
- Allocate each selected indoor use between plinth and tower and apply the
  appropriate construction rates, rather than pricing all area as plinth.
- Specify the conversion between rentable/program area and gross construction
  floor area, including common areas and circulation, or explicitly retain the
  simplified common-area basis used by the dummy model.

7. FAR bonus aggregation and allocation
---------------------------------------
- Decide how per-use bonuses combine: sum, weighted aggregation, or another rule.
- Define whether bonuses are earned and spent on the same parcel or transferred.
- Specify any parcel-wide or site-wide bonus cap and physical floor-area cap.
- Recompute bonuses from selected area and corresponding NOI; do not use the
  example catalog's precomputed bonuses for differently sized programs.
- Preserve size proportionality: with equal demand and NOI per sq ft, a larger
  program receives a proportionally larger bonus. No minimum subsidy eligibility.
- Specify whether outdoor uses also earn the existing demand/NOI-based bonus.
- Ensure the aggregation rule treats splitting identical programs consistently,
  rather than awarding an extra fixed bonus for each instance.
- Count extra building uses' income and costs through those uses themselves;
  do not also credit the originating amenity with profit from bonus floor area.
- Check that bonus-generated capacity cannot expand indefinitely. A fixed
  physical capacity cap is needed if bonus feedback would otherwise permit this.

8. Required uses, policy requirements, and compatibility
------------------------------------------------------
- Specify any required uses or minimum provision, such as housing, attainable
  housing, community facilities, or dedicated open space; otherwise they are optional.
- Specify any maximum use shares, concentration limits, or demand saturation
  limits if desired. Preference weights currently express demand, not capacity.
- Specify incompatibilities, adjacency requirements, or dependencies between
  uses if needed for valid building programs.
- If there are no such requirements, record that explicitly rather than assuming
  the optimizer will provide a balanced program on its own.

9. Optimization objective and feasibility target
-----------------------------------------------
- Choose what to maximize: annual NOI, annual surplus, demand satisfaction, or
  a defined combination. This is an objective decision, not a capacity constraint.
- If using annual surplus, specify required yield:
  annual surplus = annual NOI - required yield * development cost.
- If a minimum development yield is required, specify its threshold and cost
  scope, including whether land is included at the development level.
- Do not sum per-amenity yield percentages. Development yield is combined annual
  NOI divided by combined development cost.
- Decide whether no development is an allowed solution when options fail targets.
- For multiple objectives, specify priorities or weights and compatible units.

10. Financial conventions needed for consistent evaluation
---------------------------------------------------------
- Confirm rent periods, especially the inherited annual multifamily per-unit
  rent convention. Do not silently convert it to monthly rent.
- Specify vacancy/occupancy allowances if income should be adjusted for them.
- Confirm whether maintenance allowances and commercial opex cover all expenses
  intended in NOI, or whether additional costs must be supplied.
- Keep public/community revenue at zero under the established dummy model rule.
- Specify whether this remains a stabilized annual model or must include timing,
  construction phasing, financing, resale, and discounted cash flows.

Illustrative constraint structure (values and aggregation still unspecified)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Indoor gross area <= parcel land area * (base FAR + combined FAR bonus)
Indoor gross area <= absolute parcel floor-area limit
Outdoor improved area <= dedicated open-space allotment
Plinth area <= plinth capacity
Tower area <= tower capacity
Required ground-floor area <= available ground-floor capacity
Total scoped development cost <= scoped budget, if a budget is imposed
Selected use area is zero or lies within the applicable program bounds

These constraints must be integrated into an allocator/optimizer. The dummy
catalog currently calculates use-level costs, NOI, yield, and FAR bonuses; it
does not select a feasible parcel program or enforce the constraints above.
